st-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff2%2Fa4%2F93f4bc454764a5382d4f80cdc0f4%2F20261006-dsc06292.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2530+0+268/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff2%2Fa4%2F93f4bc454764a5382d4f80cdc0f4%2F20261006-dsc06292.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2530+0+268/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff2%2Fa4%2F93f4bc454764a5382d4f80cdc0f4%2F20261006-dsc06292.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2530+0+268/resize/800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff2%2Fa4%2F93f4bc454764a5382d4f80cdc0f4%2F20261006-dsc06292.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2530+0+268/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff2%2Fa4%2F93f4bc454764a5382d4f80cdc0f4%2F20261006-dsc06292.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2530+0+268/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff2%2Fa4%2F93f4bc454764a5382d4f80cdc0f4%2F20261006-dsc06292.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2530+0+268/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff2%2Fa4%2F93f4bc454764a5382d4f80cdc0f4%2F20261006-dsc06292.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2530+0+268/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff2%2Fa4%2F93f4bc454764a5382d4f80cdc0f4%2F20261006-dsc06292.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2530+0+268/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff2%2Fa4%2F93f4bc454764a5382d4f80cdc0f4%2F20261006-dsc06292.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2530+0+268/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff2%2Fa4%2F93f4bc454764a5382d4f80cdc0f4%2F20261006-dsc06292.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/4500x2530+0+268/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2Ff2%2Fa4%2F93f4bc454764a5382d4f80cdc0f4%2F20261006-dsc06292.jpg" class="img" alt="Environmentalist Jassim al-Asadi stands on a boat and looks out over the marshes where he grew up in southern Iraq. Despite having been kidnapped, tortured and accused by an Iran-backed militia of being an Israeli agent, Asadi is back in the marshes." loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Environmentalist Jassim al-Asadi stands on a boat and looks out over the marshes where he grew up in southern Iraq. Despite having been kidnapped, tortured and accused by an Iran-backed militia of being an Israeli agent, Asadi is back in the marshes.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Claire Harbage/NPR                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;p&gt;Iraqi environmentalist Jassim al-Asadi has dedicated his life to protecting Iraq's ancient marshlands. He even helped reflood areas that Saddam Hussein drained and set fire to decades ago to flush out resistance fighters. In February 2023, an Iran-backed militia kidnapped and tortured Asadi for two weeks on false charges that he was an Israeli spy who had ignited anti-government protests. He endured the trauma by reciting poetry until his Bani Asad tribe mobilized armed tribal fighters to secure his release. Once freed, he refused European asylum so he could continue his work. Today, despite drought and other hardships on the land, Asadi continues to monitor recovering water levels as &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/11/18/nx-s1-6043167/iraq-environmentalist-marshes-iran-backed-militia-kidnapped" target="_blank"&gt;wildlife slowly starts to return&lt;/a&gt; to the marshes.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Weekend picks&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-154133" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 3840;         --source-height: 2160;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3840x2160+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fnpr-production-npr.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com%2Fbrightspot%2F4e%2F0d%2F728b89154973a4e942ced4d58d36%2Fjason-and-lucia-03.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3840x2160+0+0/r