      <title>Colom wins Mississippi Senate race. And, White House warns Dems over future probes</title>
      <author>Brittney Melton</author>
      <link>https://www.npr.org/2026/11/12/g-s1-153171/up-first-newsletter-scott-colom-trump-democrats-ukraine-russia</link>
      <comments>https://text.npr.org/2026/11/12/g-s1-153171/up-first-newsletter-scott-colom-trump-democrats-ukraine-russia</comments>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.npr.org/2026/11/12/g-s1-153171/up-first-newsletter-scott-colom-trump-democrats-ukraine-russia</guid>
      <pubDate>Thu, 12 Nov 2026 12:15:48 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div id="storytext" class="storytext storylocation linkLocation"&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Good morning. You're reading the Up First newsletter. &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/newsletter/news" target="_blank"&gt;Subscribe&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; here to get it delivered to your inbox, and &lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/podcasts/510318/up-first/" target="_blank"&gt;listen&lt;/a&gt;&lt;em&gt; to the Up First podcast for all the news you need to start your day.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Today's top stories&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Democrat Scott Colom has won the U.S. Senate seat from Mississippi&lt;/strong&gt;, defeating Republican Sen. Cindy Hyde-Smith by just a few thousand votes. Colom will be the first Black Senator from the state in &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/11/12/nx-s1-6037438/scott-colom-defied-the-odds-mississippi-democratic-senator" target="_blank"&gt;nearly 150 years&lt;/a&gt;. His win &lt;a href="https://www.npr.org/2026/11/11/g-s1-151688/mississippi-senate-race-scott-colom" target="_blank"&gt;expands the Democratic majority&lt;/a&gt; in the new Congress to 55 seats. The Alaska race is still undecided.&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-151686" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 3174;         --source-height: 1785;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3174x1785+0+165/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe4%2F8a%2Ff54fa9dd4de4a9cae7937ebf9994%2Fmdt-colom.jpg 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3174x1785+0+165/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe4%2F8a%2Ff54fa9dd4de4a9cae7937ebf9994%2Fmdt-colom.jpg 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3