lity/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe4%2F8a%2Ff54fa9dd4de4a9cae7937ebf9994%2Fmdt-colom.jpg 800w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3174x1785+0+165/resize/900/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe4%2F8a%2Ff54fa9dd4de4a9cae7937ebf9994%2Fmdt-colom.jpg 900w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3174x1785+0+165/resize/1200/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe4%2F8a%2Ff54fa9dd4de4a9cae7937ebf9994%2Fmdt-colom.jpg 1200w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3174x1785+0+165/resize/1600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe4%2F8a%2Ff54fa9dd4de4a9cae7937ebf9994%2Fmdt-colom.jpg 1600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3174x1785+0+165/resize/1800/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe4%2F8a%2Ff54fa9dd4de4a9cae7937ebf9994%2Fmdt-colom.jpg 1800w" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3174x1785+0+165/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe4%2F8a%2Ff54fa9dd4de4a9cae7937ebf9994%2Fmdt-colom.jpg" sizes="(min-width: 1025px) 650px, calc(100vw - 30px)" class="img" type="image/webp" /&gt;             &lt;img src="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3174x1785+0+165/resize/1100/quality/50/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe4%2F8a%2Ff54fa9dd4de4a9cae7937ebf9994%2Fmdt-colom.jpg" data-template="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/3174x1785+0+165/resize/{width}/quality/{quality}/format/{format}/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe4%2F8a%2Ff54fa9dd4de4a9cae7937ebf9994%2Fmdt-colom.jpg" class="img" alt="Scott Colom speaks with attendees at an event held at the Two Mississippi Museums in Jackson, Miss. on Aug. 1, 2026. " loading="lazy" /&gt;         &lt;/picture&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div class="credit-caption"&gt;     &lt;div class="caption-wrap"&gt;         &lt;div class="caption" aria-label="Image caption"&gt;             &lt;p&gt;                 Scott Colom speaks with attendees at an event held at the Two Mississippi Museums in Jackson, Miss. on Aug. 1, 2026.                 &lt;b class="credit" aria-label="Image credit"&gt;                                          Sarah Warnock/AP                                      &lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;b class="hide-caption"&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;             &lt;/p&gt;         &lt;/div&gt;               &lt;button class="toggle-caption" aria-label=""&gt;               &lt;svg class="icon icon--info"&gt;&lt;use href="#icon-info"&gt;&lt;/use&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;               &lt;b&gt;&lt;/b&gt;                 &lt;abbr title="Toggle Caption" class="toggle-caption__info"&gt;i&lt;/abbr&gt;              &lt;/button&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;       &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;/div&gt;    &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;Colom's win highlights growing voter concerns about affordability&lt;/strong&gt; and the impact of the war in Iran, Elise Catrion Gregg of the Gulf States Newsroom and Mississippi Public Broadcasting tells &lt;em&gt;Up First&lt;/em&gt;. During her campaign, Hyde-Smith relied on her record and President Trump's support, which Gregg says is &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6038323:x" target="_blank"&gt;no longer a winning strategy&lt;/a&gt;. This race signals a potential national shift, showing that the South is becoming a viable target for Democrats, Gregg says.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;➡️ &lt;strong&gt;A U.S. Supreme Court ruling this year weakened the Voting Rights Act&lt;/strong&gt;, raising the possibility of a record drop in Black Congressional representation. The Democratic blue wave lessened the expected decline, but &lt;a href="http://npr.org/2026/11/12/nx-s1-6029640/black-voter-turnout-congress-redistricting" target="_blank"&gt;an NPR analysis shows&lt;/a&gt; that Black representation in the U.S. House is still slated to decrease with the incoming Congress.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The White House is warning Democrats that their planned investigations &lt;/strong&gt;into administration officials could cross the line into "blatant criminality." White House Counsel Will Scharf said in a letter to Rep. Robert Garcia, who is expected to lead the House Oversight Committee next year, that the administration is monitoring "abusive and capricious threats" from Democrats. The letter concludes by saying that "the administration will not be extorted and will not be cowed by unlawful process, motivated by animus, pettiness, hatred or any other unlawful motive to intimidate, penalize or interfere with the lawful functioning of government." Garcia said Democrats will not be deterred by threats from the Department of Justice or Trump's lawyers.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;The administration's arguments may buy time and push Democrats&lt;/strong&gt; to go to court to enforce their subpoenas, NPR's Tamara Keith says. She adds that the White House letter gives a preview of the legal arguments the administration might use next year. But more importantly, Keith says that it signals that the White House and Justice Department &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6038323:x" target="_blank"&gt;won't accept congressional investigations&lt;/a&gt; and oversight without a fight.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Russia and Ukraine have agreed to a significant prisoner swap &lt;/strong&gt;after two days of U.S.-mediated talks in Abu Dhabi. Trump's envoys Jared Kushner and Steve Witkoff are now in Moscow today to meet with Russian President Vladimir Putin and attempt to make more headway to end the war in Ukraine.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;aside id="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" class="mid-story-recommendations-mount-piano" aria-label="recommended stories"&gt;&lt;/aside&gt;    &lt;!-- END ID="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" CLASS="MID-STORY-RECOMMENDATIONS-MOUNT-PIANO" ARIA-LABEL="RECOMMENDED STORIES" --&gt;    &lt;ul class="edTag"&gt;&lt;li&gt;🎧 &lt;strong&gt;NPR's Charles Maynes says that, going into the talks in Abu Dhabi, U.S. officials were exploring &lt;/strong&gt;Article 5-like guarantees for Ukraine, similar to NATO's collective security defense. A move like this &lt;a href="http://one.npr.org/i/nx-s1-6038323:x" target="_blank"&gt;would be significant if implemented&lt;/a&gt;, but Moscow has already rejected the concept of a "NATO lite" status for Ukraine and its full NATO membership. Kushner said that, in Moscow, he aims to brief Putin directly and persuade him to involve Europeans in discussions about European security. European leaders, concerned about potential deals made without their input, are expected to join Kushner and Witkoff after these talks, when they'll meet with Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy in Ireland.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &lt;h3 class="edTag"&gt;Watch this&lt;/h3&gt;   &lt;div id="resg-s1-153143" class="bucketwrap image large"&gt;             &lt;div     class="imagewrap has-source-dimensions"     data-crop-type=""     style="         --source-width: 2600;         --source-height: 1463;     " &gt;         &lt;picture&gt;             &lt;source srcset="https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2600x1463+0+0/resize/400/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F40%2F16%2F696df430457cbaa5854ae5737f38%2Fnewsmakers-abdulel-sayed-clean-1.png 400w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2600x1463+0+0/resize/600/quality/85/format/webp/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F40%2F16%2F696df430457cbaa5854ae5737f38%2Fnewsmakers-abdulel-sayed-clean-1.png 600w, https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2600x1463+0+0/res